    "theme": "Food Still Life",
    "time": "2 class periods",
    "gradeBand": "Grades 6-8",
    "recommendedGrade": "Grade 8",
    "objective": "Students will use wire-line sculpture to create a food still life while demonstrating line in space, balance, and contour.",
    "artConcepts": [
      "line in space",
      "balance",
      "contour"
    ],
    "materials": [
      "recycled cardboard",
      "school glue or masking tape",
      "pipe cleaners or soft craft wire",
      "colored pencils or washable markers"
    ],
    "preparation": [
      "Prepare one finished teacher sample or technique swatch without requiring students to copy a single solution.",
      "Place shared materials in table trays and pre-cut only the items that are unsafe or too time-consuming for Grades 6-8.",
      "Display one age-appropriate visual reference connected to food traditions and observation of everyday objects."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Look closely at references for food traditions and observation of everyday objects. Name at least three shapes, textures, or visual clues that could help show a food still life.",
      "Make two small thumbnail plans. Choose the plan with the clearest focal point and strongest use of space.",
      "Prepare the work area and test wire-line sculpture on scrap material before beginning the final piece.",
      "Bend one continuous line into the main form and anchor it to a simple base.",
      "Use the technique to construct a food still life, including overlapping objects, shape, texture, highlights, and shadows.",
      "Pause halfway for a partner check: identify the focal point, one successful choice, and one area that needs stronger contrast or structure.",
      "Revise, add only the details that strengthen the idea, and clean or secure all edges and joins.",
      "Display the finished work with a two-sentence artist statement explaining the technique and one intentional choice."
    ],
    "finishedExample": "A successful result clearly communicates a food still life, uses wire-line sculpture across the composition, and includes overlapping objects, shape, texture, highlights, and shadows. Student solutions should vary in color, arrangement, and personal details.",
    "teachingTips": [
      "Demonstrate the technique for no more than five minutes, then let students test it on scrap material.",
      "Ask “What do you want the viewer to notice first?” instead of prescribing one correct appearance.",
      "Use a midpoint gallery walk so students can borrow strategies without copying complete designs.",
      "Require a thumbnail plan, purposeful technique, revision, and a short artist statement."
    ],
    "differentiation": [
      "Support: offer a limited material tray, a visual step card, and a partially prepared base.",
      "Extension: require a second focal area, a more complex spatial solution, or an additional technique layer.",
      "Accessibility: allow adaptive scissors, larger tools, pre-torn materials, a partner role, or digital planning before making."
    ],
    "assessment": [
      "Technique: the student uses wire-line sculpture with growing control.",
      "Idea: the artwork communicates a clear interpretation of food still life.",
      "Craft: materials are intentionally arranged, attached, or finished.",
      "Reflection: the student identifies one choice, one challenge, and one possible revision."
    ],
    "vocabulary": [
      "Wire-Line Sculpture",
      "line in space",
      "balance",
      "contour"
    ],
    "crossCurricular": "Connect the project to food traditions and observation of everyday objects. Invite students to collect two facts or observations before planning their artwork.",
    "lowMaterialPlan": "Run this as a shared-material station. Replace specialty supplies with paper scraps, cardboard packaging, pencil, washable color, and school glue while preserving the core goal: line in space, balance, and contour.",
    "cleanup": "Reserve the final 7 minutes. Return reusable tools, place wet or dimensional work on a labeled drying surface, recycle clean scraps, and wipe tables.",
